fix: use streaming backup to prevent "Invalid string length" for large datasets

Users with large databases (e.g., a year of chat history) hit V8's ~512MB
string length limit when JSON.stringify() is called on the entire IndexedDB
in getBackupData(). This causes "Invalid string length" errors even with
the simplified backup option enabled.

Replace the single-shot JSON.stringify + IPC transfer with a streaming
approach: the renderer reads IndexedDB tables in batches (500 records),
serializes each batch individually, and streams chunks to the main process
via multiple small IPC calls. The main process writes chunks directly to
a file stream, never holding the entire dataset in memory.

Key changes:
- Add streaming IPC channels: createDataWriter, writeDataChunk,
  closeDataWriter, backupFromStream
- New streamBackupData() in BackupService that writes data.json
  incrementally via IPC chunked writes
- BackupManager gains streaming writer methods with backpressure support
- Extract _compressAndFinalize() to share ZIP logic between legacy and
  streaming paths
- Migrate all backup paths (local, WebDAV, S3, Nutstore, LAN transfer)
  to use streaming
- data.json format is unchanged — restore needs zero modifications

Fixes CherryHQ/cherry-studio#11862
